## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design a plug-in style, runtime-extensible CLI using classic design patterns with a minimal surface area.

## Key Concepts

- **Registry Pattern**: Maps command names to factories for dynamic lookup
- **Factory Pattern**: Creates command instances on demand
- **Dispatcher**: Resolves and executes commands with provided arguments
- **Conflict Handling**: Strategy-based approach (overwrite, warn, or reject)
- **Help Generation**: Aggregates help() output from all registered commands

## Code Example

```typescript
interface Command {
  execute(args: string[]): Promise<any>;
  help(): string;
}

type CommandFactory = () => Command;

class CommandRegistry {
  private factories = new Map<string, CommandFactory>();

  register(name: string, factory: CommandFactory, allowOverwrite = false): void {
    if (this.factories.has(name) && !allowOverwrite) {
      throw new Error(`Command '${name}' already registered`);
    }
    this.factories.set(name, factory);
  }

  getFactory(name: string): CommandFactory | undefined {
    return this.factories.get(name);
  }

  getAllCommands(): string[] {
    return Array.from(this.factories.keys());
  }
}

class Dispatcher {
  constructor(private registry: CommandRegistry) {}

  async dispatch(name: string, args: string[]): Promise<any> {
    const factory = this.registry.getFactory(name);
    if (!factory) {
      throw new Error(`Unknown command: ${name}`);
    }
    return factory().execute(args);
  }

  help(): string {
    const commands = this.registry.getAllCommands();
    return commands.map(name => {
      const factory = this.registry.getFactory(name)!;
      return factory().help();
    }).join('\n');
  }
}

// Usage:
const registry = new CommandRegistry();
const dispatcher = new Dispatcher(registry);

registry.register('build', () => ({ execute: async () => { /*...*/ }, help: () => 'build: Build the project' }));

await dispatcher.dispatch('build', ['--prod']);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of runtime extensibility, plugin architecture, and composition patterns in a practical context.

## Key Concepts
- **Pipeline vs Chain of Responsibility:** ordered transformation versus conditional delegation
- Immutable registries for thread-safety
- Per-item isolation and error containment across stages

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Formatter {
  transform(line: string): string;
}

class Registry {
  private map = new Map<string, () => Formatter>();

  register(name: string, factory: () => Formatter): void {
    this.map.set(name, factory);
  }

  create(name: string): Formatter | undefined {
    const factory = this.map.get(name);
    return factory ? factory() : undefined;
  }
}

class Pipeline {
  private formatters: Formatter[] = [];

  use(formatter: Formatter): this {
    this.formatters.push(formatter);
    return this;
  }

  execute(line: string): string {
    return this.formatters.reduce(
      (acc, formatter) => formatter.transform(acc),
      line
    );
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new Registry();
registry.register('timestamp', () => ({
  transform: (line) => `[${Date.now()}] ${line}`
}));
registry.register('emoji', () => ({
  transform: (line) => `${line} 🪵`
}));

const pipeline = new Pipeline();
pipeline.use(registry.create('timestamp')!);
pipeline.use(registry.create('emoji')!);

console.log(pipeline.execute('log message'));
// Output: [1712345678900] log message 🪵
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a robust, runtime-extensible scheduling system that supports multiple job types and dynamic plug-ins without touching core executor.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for scheduling policies
- Registry/Plugin architecture for runtime extension
- Concurrency, thread-safety, and asynchronous dispatch
- Type-safe factories and minimal interfaces in TypeScript

## Code Example
```typescript
type JobType = 'cpu'|'io'|'delay'
interface Job {
  id: string
  type: JobType
  priority?: number
  payload?: any
  run: () => Promise<void>
}
interface Scheduler {
  schedule(job: Job): Promise<void>
}
type Factory = () => Scheduler
class SchedulerRegistry {
  private static factories = new Map<string, Factory>()
  static register(name: string, factory: Factory){ this.factories.set(name, factory) }
  static create(name: string): Scheduler {
    const f = this.factories.get(name)
    if (!f) throw new Error(`Unknown scheduler: ${name}`)
    return f()
  }
}
class PriorityScheduler implements Scheduler {
  private queue: Job[] = []
  async schedule(job: Job): Promise<void> {
    this.queue.push(job)
    this.queue.sort((a,b)=> (b.priority ?? 0) - (a.priority ?? 0))
    // pretend execution
    const head = this.queue.shift()
    if (head) await head.run()
  }
}
// Wiring
SchedulerRegistry.register('priority', ()=> new PriorityScheduler())
```

